# jaaOS dual-env PAM wiring (prototype).
#
# Add these to the services that should select/verify an environment: login, sshd,
# gdm-password, sudo, su, su-l, polkit-1. (A production build ships this as a
# /usr/share/pam-configs profile so `pam-auth-update` manages ordering.)
#
# ---- AUTH ----
# pam_exec runs our helper and feeds the typed password on stdin (expose_authtok).
# [success=1 default=ignore] makes us effectively "sufficient" ONLY on an affirmative
# exit 0 from the helper: success jumps over pam_deny (authenticated); ANY other result
# is ignored and control falls through to the normal stack (pam_unix), which fails on the
# locked account. The helper is written to exit non-zero for non-managed users and wrong
# passwords precisely so this cannot become an auth bypass. Order: place BEFORE pam_unix.
#
auth    [success=1 default=ignore]  pam_exec.so  expose_authtok quiet  /usr/lib/jaa-env/bin/jaa-env-pam
auth    requisite                   pam_deny.so
auth    required                    pam_permit.so
# ...existing distro auth lines (pam_unix.so etc.) continue below unchanged...

# ---- SESSION ----
# Mount the unlocked env's home, then let pam_namespace give this session a fresh
# per-session tmpfs /tmp and /var/tmp (destroyed at logout -> nothing survives an env
# switch). [default=ignore] so a helper hiccup never blocks the session teardown.
#
session [default=ignore]  pam_exec.so  quiet  /usr/lib/jaa-env/bin/jaa-env-pam
session required          pam_namespace.so
# ...existing distro session lines (pam_systemd.so etc.) continue below...
